I had some looks and found this change was merged https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/pull/95561 to v1.20 which explains the difference.

The reason this happens at all is because we were not able to catch the container termination when it happened, we just know it used to exist and now is gone - so we know it has been terminated but don't really know why.

We can expect this kind of situation for example when our container has been killed by something other than kubelet and kubelet missed it, so generally saying @bydga I think it's a bit risky to assume you could always get the container's exit code because these things can happen from time to time.

Having say that, it's strange that we see this happen when the termination is "planned", i.e when it is being triggered from a deployment controller. Need further investiagtion on that, but as I was able to reproduce it only once, it's a bit hard to see where it falls. @bydga It would be very helpful if you could provide some detailed kubelet logs (verbosity  level 5) and maybe some logs from your container runtime - around when this issue occurs so I could keep looking into it. My best guess here is the container termination cycle happens before the kubelet's PLEG's is able to report the termination event (it is by default configured to 1s - see https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/355bc3df228d9529a15e53d4bb59757e191c0225/pkg/kubelet/kubelet.go#L158-L166)
Also, I would try to upgrade to v1.20 to see if it still happens frequently as described

